doc.push(PB("The shortcut was to reuse the reviewer account table chat-6 had just built. It is the wrong shape, and the reason is the domain rather than the code. A reviewer is a person: the session expires in hours and dies if the certificate behind it is revoked, because a reviewer who has left should stop recording decisions immediately. A collector is scheduled software on a wayside detector. It runs for weeks, nobody signs it in, and revoking an enrollment because one detector was decommissioned should not break a running collector mid-shift without an operator deciding to. One credential table for both would have made the two lifecycles share a single fate, and the auditor's question — is this the same credential — would have had no clean answer."));
doc.push(PB("So `backend/ingest_auth.py` gives collectors their own register. Enrollment is by name, with no certification binding. The token is 32 random bytes, returned once, of which only the SHA-256 digest is stored. It has **no TTL**, and revoking an enrollment does not invalidate tokens already issued — it stops new ones, which is the operation an operator actually wants. The register is append-only and hash-chained with `BEFORE UPDATE` and `BEFORE DELETE` triggers, so an enrollment or a revocation cannot be edited out of history."));

doc.push(H3("11.2 — The gate that was added in the wrong place"));
doc.push(PB("The first version of the HTTP handler authenticated **after** reading and parsing the body, and the malformed-JSON branch logged the body on the way in. The combination meant an **anonymous caller could write rows to the ingest log by POSTing garbage**: send `<<<not json>>>`, the auth check is still downstream, the malformed branch fires, a row lands. Adding the credential without moving that branch would have left a write path open to anybody. The gate now runs **before the body is read at all**, and a test posts garbage with no token and asserts the ingest log still holds zero rows."));
doc.push(callout(
  "The same shape as chat-6, in a different costume",
  "The control existed. It was in the wrong place relative to the thing it was meant to protect. Chat-6's guard ran and its result was discarded; chat-7's check ran after the write it was guarding. Both were green, both were found by asking what happens to a hostile input rather than what happens to a valid one.",
  { fill: "FBEAEA", labelColor: RED },
));

doc.push(H3("11.3 — The signed reviewer key directory"));
doc.push(PB("The attestation scheme chat-5 built has a structural property the audit stated plainly: the HMAC covers the row's `record_hash`, not the reviewer name, so the person holding a key can relabel their own attestation with somebody else's name. What stops them is that the verifier is told which reviewer each key belongs to and compares. Until chat-7 that knowledge lived on a command line — `--reviewer-key KEYID=NAME:path` — which is a name typed by a human with no signature behind it."));
doc.push(PB("`backend/key_directory.py` publishes the bindings as a signed document: reviewer, cert_id, key_id, registration time and revocation time for every registry row, including the revocations. It carries **no key material** — a key_id is the first sixteen hex of the key's SHA-256, which lets a recipient match a key without revealing anything that would let anyone forge an attestation. `tools/publish_key_directory.py` builds it on the Platform host and refuses if the registry chain does not verify; `tools/verify_key_directory.py` verifies it on a recipient machine importing nothing from `backend/`, the same rule the release verifier follows, and emits the `KEY_ID=REVIEWER:` fragments ready for `verify_ledger_export.py`. The recipient never types a name."));
doc.push(callout(
  "The signature is the weak guard here, and the directory knows it",
  "The directory is signed with the same symmetric HMAC key the bundles use, so whoever can verify it can also sign one. A signature therefore establishes that the export-key holder authored the list, not that they were entitled to. The guard that actually constrains an attacker who can sign their own directory is **consistency**: one key_id may name exactly one reviewer for its whole life. The test that matters forges a directory — one key under a second name — re-signs it so the signature passes, and the directory is still refused, by `entries_consistent`. A signature proves who wrote the file; the consistency check constrains what the file may say.",
  { fill: "FFF4E0", labelColor: AMBER },
));

doc.push(H3("11.4 — The bookkeeping defect, and what it cost"));
doc.push(PB("Two test files were written without the house `@test` decorator and `main()` function. The runner imports each module and calls `main()`; these had none, so it printed the section header and **ran zero tests** — while the aggregate total rose from 326 to 340 on tests that had never executed. Two commit messages quoted the inflated numbers. It was caught by reading the per-file PASS count, not by the exit status: the suite said `340 passed, 0 failed`. Both files were rewritten in the house pattern and now run 15 and 15; the corrected total is 346."));
doc.push(PB("The rule the stage adds: **a new test file is verified by its own per-file PASS count, never by the suite total moving.** It is the same family as the vacuous ledger test, the self-referential ROI test and the inert guard — a green number that means less than it appears to. Four sessions, four different ways for a passing suite to be passing about nothing."));

doc.push(H3("11.5 — Mutation testing, and the four guards the tests never covered"));
doc.push(PB("`assets/mutation-ingest-auth.py` applies eighteen mutations to the ingest gate and the key directory and records whether any test goes red. **Fourteen were killed. Four survived** — meaning the guard could be deleted and the suite would stay green. The four are M2 (the missing-token check, because no test sends a request with no `Authorization` header to an enrolled deployment), M3 (`resolve_token` raising inside the handler), M5 (a direct call to the route function bypassing the header check), and M9 (the `entry_hash` comparison, because the existing tamper test trips the `prev_hash` check instead)."));
doc.push(PB("These are test gaps, not code defects: the guards are present, they are simply not exercised in the way each mutation disables them. Each is written into the chat-7 carry-forward with the exact test to add, and a fix is verified by re-running the driver, which exits zero only when every mutation is killed. The driver keeps a pristine snapshot in `/tmp` and a `--restore` flag, because a run cut off mid-mutation leaves the tree modified and `git checkout --` has destroyed uncommitted work in this repository before."));

doc.push(H3("11.6 — Verification for the stage"));
doc.push(P("Suite 315 to 346, after the two silent files were repaired. Eighteen mutations attempted against the two new guards; fourteen killed and four reported as findings rather than as passes. Documents updated in the same commits as the code: B01 §7 items 1 and 5, the AD-2 architecture row, D09 §4.3's authentication and data-validation rows, and D09 §4.5.5 — the last because the reviewer key directory changes what a recipient of a bundle needs to be told. Two build records were written, `assets/ingest-auth-build-2026-11-11.md` and `assets/key-directory-build-2026-11-11.md`."));
doc.push(P("The stage does not close item 1 or item 5. What keeps item 1 open is not the software's behaviour but the human process behind a credential — key custody and joiner/mover/leaver — plus attestation at entry. What keeps item 5 open is the real payload schema, which must be agreed with the Railroad. Neither is a thing a chat can finish, and the docket says so in the item's own last column rather than in a footnote."));

'''

c = c.replace(anchor, stage11 + anchor, 1)

old_close = "Stage 10 is chat-6, and it is the shortest demonstration of the pattern: a revocation guard written to close a race, called by a route that passed it a boolean, and tested only with a guard that raised — so the guard was inert and 310 tests were green."
new_close = old_close + " Stage 11 is chat-7: two AD-2 halves built, one of them gated *after* the write it was guarding, and a mutation run in which four of eighteen guards could be deleted with the suite still green. The pattern has now held across five sessions, and the varying part is only which surface the green number was lying about."
assert old_close in c
c = c.replace(old_close, new_close, 1)

with open(p,"w",encoding="utf-8",newline="") as f: f.write(c)
print("Stage 11 inserted")
